  "query_preview": "..\n\nRest of Today through Sunday...\n\nSunny skies and a southerly breeze this afternoon will push\ntemperatures into the mid to upper 70s. A warmer night in store\ntonight under mostly clear skies. Winds will shift from southwest\nto west overnight with speeds increasing over the mountains and\nfoothills. This will keep the atmosphere more stirred up tonight\nholding temperatures 10 degrees warmer in the valleys that dropped\ninto the 30s this morning. \n\nThe breezy overnight conditions will give an early start to the\nwarming for Monday. 4-Corners ridge axis shifts over the area\ntomorrow with building heights, while at the same time the height\ngradient tightens overhead driving stronger westerly flow aloft.\nThis downslope gradient combined with building heights and plenty\nof sunshine will push temperatures toward record readings across\nthe area Monday afternoon. Record highs are possible for Billings,\nSheridan, and Livingston. Baker and Miles City are forecast to be\n4 to 5 degrees under their record levels of 89 and 90\nrespectively. NBM is showing the potential for low lying areas\nfrom Billings to the southeast into NE Wyoming to reach 90+\ndegrees, with probabilities ranging from around 20% in Billings,\nto 55% in Sheridan, and 30 to 50 percent over southern Powder \nRiver and Carter Counties. \n\nFire weather concerns are elevated for Monday across the forecast\narea due to the unseasonably warm temperatures, low Relative \nHumidities, and Gusty westerly winds (20-35 mph). Along the \nwestern foothills conditions will be critical as this area has \nseen much less precipitation over the past month than areas \nfurther to the east, so the fuels (especially grasses) are more \nfavorable to carry fire there. Will upgrade the Fire Weather Watch\nin this area to a Red Flag Warning from 10am to 8pm Monday. Use\nextra caution with anything that could start a fire tomorrow. \n\nA disturbance crossing NC Canada will push a weak dry cold front\nthrough the area Tuesday. Other than shifting winds to a northerly\ndirection, the slightly cooler air will push highs back down into\nthe 70s. \n\n4-Corners ridge nudges back into the area Wednesday and Thursday\nkeeping sunny dry and unseasonably warm temperatures in place.\nHighs remain in the 70s to around 80 degrees each day. Strong\nmixing will bring breezy conditions at times during the warmest\npart of the day. \n\nFriday is looking more and more like a pre-frontal day with models\ntrending toward a frontal passage late Friday night. As such\ntemperatures and wind speeds are trending higher Friday afternoon.\nLooking at the NBM 90th percentile shows temperatures similar to\nMondays forecast, with potentially stronger wind gusts across the\narea. Will be monitoring this period closely for potential fire\nweather highlights through this week. At this point the NBM\ndeterministic is showing highs in the lower 80s with dry and\nbreezy conditions Friday afternoon. \n\nAs mentioned above, the models are trending toward a significant\npattern shift for next weekend. Starting with a cold frontal\npassage late Friday night, the latest cluster analysis shows 68\npercent of ensemble members are showing an upper trof over the\narea on Saturday, climbing to 93 percent for Sunday, and 87\npercent on Monday. The alternate pattern shows a storm system\nstaying over the NorCal area with warm but unsettled southwesterly\nflow over the area. Both of these patterns generate precipitation\nfor our area, however the favored colder pattern brings\nsignificantly more precipitation, along with accumulating mountain\nsnowfall. Just keep in mind that Fall is a tough time for model\naccuracy in the extended due to the atmosphere being in flux, and\nthe conditions in the tropical Pacific (numerous\nTyphoons/Hurricanes) can have oversized influences on our weather\nover weekly timespans. So, despite the increase confidence in a\nshift to cooler and wetter conditions for next weekend, there is\nstill plenty of time for changes to present themselves. Those with\noutdoor plans for next weekend, especially in the high country,\nshould pay attention to the forecast through the week ahead.\nChambers",
